Paper Summary:
This paper studies GUI grounding in autoregressive multimodal GUI agents. The core claim is that current GUI agents conflate two computations: spatially localizing the intended UI element and serializing that location into coordinate/action tokens. The authors argue that these two computations peak at different transformer depths: intermediate layers contain more decodable instruction-conditioned spatial information, whereas later layers better predict coordinate tokens under teacher forcing. They call this mismatch the “coordinate serialization bottleneck.” The paper supports this claim using a frozen-backbone spatial probe and a parameter-free coordinate-token likelihood “serialization lens” across four GUI agents based on Qwen2.5-VL and Qwen3-VL backbones. The abstract and introduction state this as the main phenomenon and motivate a frozen-backbone retrofit, ZwerGe-UI.

Methodologically, ZwerGe-UI attaches independent cross-attention probes to intermediate transformer layers, producing per-layer spatial posteriors over visual patch tokens, and then optionally learns a lightweight cross-layer fusion head to combine these posteriors. The backbone is kept frozen, and the method avoids autoregressive coordinate-token decoding at inference, instead decoding from a heatmap/patch posterior.

Empirically, the paper evaluates on five GUI grounding benchmarks: ScreenSpot-Pro, ScreenSpot-v2, OSWorld-G, MMBench-GUI, and UI-Vision, using four GUI agents: GUI-Owl-7B, UI-TARS-1.5-7B, GUI-Owl-1.5-8B, and UI-Venus-1.5-8B. The experimental setup reports 200k training examples for the probe/fusion heads, frozen backbones, and patch-granularity overlap@1 evaluation. The results show substantial gains for some Qwen2.5-based models and some task families, especially OSWorld-G, MMBench-GUI, and UI-Vision spatial tasks, but also mixed or negative results for stronger Qwen3-based baselines on several benchmarks.

Overall, the paper is about probing and exploiting intermediate-layer spatial representations in GUI agents, with a practical frozen-backbone retrofit motivated by an analysis of the depth mismatch between localization and coordinate serialization.

Summary Of Strengths:
Timely and relevant problem. GUI grounding is central to current computer-use and GUI-agent systems. The paper targets a concrete weakness of coordinate-generating agents: the possibility that spatial localization and coordinate serialization are different computational stages. This is a useful framing for the GUI-agent community.
Interesting empirical hypothesis. The proposed “coordinate serialization bottleneck” is a plausible and potentially important phenomenon. The layerwise probing results, instruction-switch counterfactual, and high-resolution/small-target analysis provide an appealing diagnostic story: intermediate representations may be better suited for spatial grounding than the final representation used for token generation. The four stated findings are clearly organized and connected to the method.
The retrofit idea is practically attractive. Freezing the backbone and attaching relatively small grounding heads is appealing because it could make existing GUI agents more usable without expensive end-to-end retraining. The method is also conceptually simple: read an anchor representation, cross-attend to visual patch tokens, and fuse layerwise heatmaps.
Evaluation spans multiple models and benchmarks. The paper evaluates four GUI agents and five benchmarks, including high-resolution professional GUI grounding, real computer-task grounding, and multi-platform GUI evaluation. This breadth is a strength compared with papers that only report a single model or dataset.
Some results are strong and informative. The gains on UI-Vision spatial tasks are particularly large for the two Qwen2.5-based models, e.g., +25.95 pp Spatial for GUI-Owl-7B and +13.39 pp Spatial for UI-TARS-1.5-7B. This supports the idea that the method is especially helpful for tasks requiring spatial posterior recovery.
The paper is generally well organized. The narrative from diagnostic analysis to method to evaluation is easy to follow. The appendices include additional layerwise curves and qualitative cases, which help readers understand the claimed inverted-U spatial profile.
Summary Of Weaknesses:
The main performance claim is overstated relative to the tables. The paper repeatedly suggests that ZWERGE-UI improves GUI grounding across benchmarks, but the main results are mixed. On ScreenSpot-Pro, ZWERGE improves GUI-Owl-7B and UI-TARS-1.5-7B, but substantially hurts GUI-Owl-1.5-8B and UI-Venus-1.5-8B. On ScreenSpot-v2, three of the four retrofits are slightly or clearly worse than their baselines. On MMBench-GUI, UI-Venus-1.5-8B is worse after retrofitting. The paper does acknowledge that Qwen2.5 backbones benefit more than Qwen3 backbones, but the abstract/conclusion should more clearly state that the method is not consistently beneficial and may degrade stronger coordinate-trained models.
The “bottleneck” interpretation is suggestive but not yet fully established. The spatial lens uses supervised probes with around 42M parameters trained on 200k grounding examples. Even with the backbone frozen, a full-rank cross-attention probe can learn substantial task-specific readout geometry and dataset priors. Therefore, the result primarily shows that target location is more decodable from some intermediate layers under this probe architecture; it does not by itself prove that final layers have lost spatial information or that late layers are “coordinate serializers, not spatial refiners.” The instruction-switch counterfactual helps, but it uses only 150 pairs and still depends on the trained probe. More controls are needed: lower-capacity linear probes, shared probes across layers, random-label controls, held-out OOD UI layouts, probe-capacity sweeps, and direct comparison with attention-only or saliency baselines.
The serialization lens needs more methodological detail and controls. The paper measures coordinate token log-likelihood at each layer using the native LM head under teacher forcing. However, applying a final LM head to intermediate states is a logit-lens-style analysis that can be heavily confounded by layerwise alignment to the output embedding space. It is expected that later layers will become more compatible with the final LM head, even for reasons unrelated to coordinate serialization. The paper should specify exactly how intermediate logits are computed, whether final norm/output head are used unchanged, and should include controls such as non-coordinate action tokens, random coordinates, text-only tokens, calibrated tuned lenses, or causal interventions showing that intermediate spatial peaks actually improve coordinate prediction when routed to a decoder.
There appears to be a technical inconsistency in the Stage 2 objective. The paper states that in Stage 2 all backbone and probe weights are frozen, and only fusion parameters are updated. It then adds an auxiliary per-layer KL loss and says this prevents fusion from collapsing by keeping all active probe posteriors individually calibrated. But if the per-layer posteriors (p_\ell) come from frozen probes and are not functions of the fusion parameters, this auxiliary term is constant with respect to the trained parameters and cannot have the claimed effect. The authors need to clarify whether gradients actually flow into the probes in Stage 2, whether (p_\ell) changes through the fusion module, or whether this auxiliary loss should be removed/reinterpreted.
Training data and leakage controls are under-specified. The implementation section says Stage 1 trains on 200k grounding samples for one epoch and Stage 2 uses the same data, but the paper does not sufficiently describe where these samples come from, whether they include any benchmark data, how duplicates or near-duplicates are removed, how screenshots/instructions are normalized, or whether test-set UI states might overlap with training data. This is a serious reproducibility and validity issue, especially because GUI benchmarks can contain visually similar screenshots and because the proposed probes are supervised.
The evaluation metric may not reflect actual click success and may favor patch-level methods. The paper reports patch-granularity overlap@1, treating baseline coordinates as patch centers and checking whether the resulting patch overlaps the ground-truth box. This can give credit to predictions whose patch overlaps a small target even if the actual click point is not inside the element. Since ZWERGE-UI is inherently patch-level, this metric is not enough to evaluate actual GUI action execution. The paper should also report standard point-in-box click accuracy at native resolution, distance-to-target, possibly IoU or center-error, and should clearly state patch size/resolution for each model and benchmark.
The diagnostic sample sizes and uncertainty reporting are insufficient. The key layerwise analysis in Figure 1 uses (n=200), and the instruction-switch experiment uses (n=150) pairs. Several reported gaps are on the order of 6–11 percentage points, where binomial uncertainty and seed variance may matter. The paper should report confidence intervals or bootstrap intervals for hit@1, coordinate log-likelihood, suppression, JS divergence, and collapse gaps, and should report whether the probe training is stable across random seeds.
Appendix claims about fusion seem stronger than the plotted evidence. The appendix states that fusion “consistently outperforms the best single layer” and that the fusion dotted line lies above the peak of the per-layer curve in nearly all panels. However, several plots appear to show fusion below or close to the best individual probe, especially in ScreenSpot-Pro and ScreenSpot-v2. The authors should correct this claim and provide a quantitative table comparing best single layer, average over active layers, Stage 1-only, and Stage 2 fusion.
The ablations do not directly validate the final method. The ablation section uses a “fast LoRA-probe variant” separate from the final CrossAttn probes, trained on only 50k samples and evaluated at checkpoint 800. Moreover, some ablation results seem to undermine the final design choices: for example, Gaussian+CosMeta is not consistently better than alternatives, and OSWorld-G drops sharply for several variants. The final CrossAttn architecture should be ablated directly, including probe capacity, number of active layers, final-layer-only probe, intermediate-only probe, static averaging, learned fusion without CosMeta, and patch decoding strategy.
Important related baselines should be discussed more concretely. GUI-Actor is especially close: it is also a coordinate-free GUI grounding method with an attention-based action head and frozen-backbone claims, and reports ScreenSpot-Pro results with Qwen2/Qwen2.5 backbones. Re-Prefill is also directly relevant because it studies the prefill stage and evaluates on the same family of GUI grounding benchmarks. The paper cites these lines of work, but the experimental comparison and positioning are not yet sufficient for a selective ACL venue. (arXiv)
The paper needs clearer distinction between analysis contribution and method contribution. The diagnostic findings are interesting even if ZWERGE-UI is not consistently stronger than baselines. Conversely, if the method is presented as the main contribution, the mixed results and missing comparison to close coordinate-free methods are more problematic. The authors should decide whether the paper is primarily an interpretability/analysis paper or a method paper, and align claims, evaluation, and title accordingly.
Comments Suggestions And Typos:
Please temper broad claims such as “improves GUI grounding across five benchmarks.” A more accurate statement would be that ZWERGE-UI substantially helps some Qwen2.5-based models and spatial/layout-heavy tasks, while gains are mixed or negative for stronger Qwen3-based coordinate-trained models.
Add a table summarizing deltas by model and benchmark, including number of wins/losses and average delta for Qwen2.5 vs Qwen3 backbones.
Provide full details of the 200k training samples: dataset names, licenses, split construction, filtering, duplicate removal, benchmark overlap checks, screenshot resolution distribution, instruction templates, and whether any evaluation benchmark training portions were used.
Report standard native point-in-box accuracy in addition to patch overlap@1. If patch overlap@1 is retained, clearly define patch size, grid construction, and how patch predictions are converted to executable click coordinates.
Include statistical uncertainty: confidence intervals for all main tables, bootstrap intervals for layerwise curves, and at least 3 random seeds for probe/fusion training.
Clarify the Stage 2 loss. As written, the auxiliary loss appears constant if probes are frozen. If this is a writing error, state exactly which parameters receive gradients.
Clarify the serialization lens. Does it use the final RMSNorm and LM head? Are hidden states normalized before the LM head? How are coordinate-token sequences selected and aligned? Are x and y tokens evaluated separately? Does coordinate log-likelihood correlate with actual coordinate accuracy?
Add stronger controls for the probe analysis: random instruction, random target, shuffled labels, image-only, text-only, saliency-only, final-layer tuned probe with higher capacity, and lower-capacity linear probes.
The explanation that Qwen3-VL backbones have more diverse grounding supervision is plausible but should be supported with evidence or removed/softened.
The OSWorld-G refusal removal should include the number and percentage of removed samples, and whether refusal removal changes the task distribution.
Please make macro vs micro averaging explicit. Some table/figure differences may be due to macro vs sample-weighted averages, but this is not always clear.
Typo/grammar: “This pattern is consistent with the our hypothesis” → “This pattern is consistent with our hypothesis.”
Grammar: “This normalization ensures that, neither system is penalized ...” → “This normalization ensures that neither system is penalized ...”
Naming should be consistent: the paper uses “ZWERGE-UI,” “ZwerGe,” and “ZwerGe-UI.” Choose one style.
Equation 5 should explicitly define score_l, not only describe it in prose.
The paper should include compute/inference-time overhead: number of hooked layers, memory overhead, latency relative to autoregressive decoding, and whether all active layers must be retained during the forward pass.
